# Names the real `omarchy-update` calls in its ordinary flow that must not run
# on this box, and must not refuse loudly either: a deny stub exits 2 and
# `omarchy-update` runs under `set -e`, so refusing takes the whole update
# with it. A skip stub does nothing and exits 0.
#
# This is a third answer, distinct from both the others. bin.deny is "nothing
# should ever call this, and if something does, say so". bin.wrap is "this has
# to behave differently here". bin.skip is "upstream is right to call this,
# and here it has nothing to do".
#
# Every row needs the reason it cannot simply run, because a row whose reason
# has lapsed is one that should go back to being real.

# Exits 0 only when OMARCHY_PATH is /usr/share/omarchy. Ours is the working
# prefix, which is not a git checkout, so it reaches "Error: OMARCHY_PATH is
# not a git checkout" and exits 1.
omarchy-update-dev

# Held back rather than dropped: monarchy_after_omarchy_update runs the real
# one once the overlay has been rebuilt. It does four things besides restart
# the shell -- prompts to reboot when the running kernel is no longer the
# installed one, prompts on reboot-required, prompts when Hyprland's binary
# reads (deleted), and sweeps the restart-*-required markers -- and monarchy
# has no equivalent for any of them. Now that `omarchy update` owns -Syu,
# kernel upgrades happen here, so the reboot prompt matters more than it did.
#
# It cannot run where upstream puts it. A reboot taken there is taken after
# the upgrade and before monarchy has rebuilt the overlay, so the box comes
# back with the session Exec= and the greeter still belonging to the old
# tree. And the shell restart would land on stock QML and be repeated moments
# later. Afterwards both are right, and it owns the shell restart at that
# point: monarchy_restart_shell stands down via MONARCHY_DEFER_SHELL_RESTART.
omarchy-update-restart

# Migrations are classified before they are allowed to run: migrations.deny
# halts an apply until a new hazardous one has been looked at, and the marker
# that keeps a denied one out of --pending is written during apply. Upstream
# runs this immediately after the upgrade that ships it, which is before
# monarchy has had either chance. Monarchy runs the real one itself, after
# classification.
omarchy-migrate
